<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Serbia</h1><p class="st-meta">1815 CE – 1882 CE · capital Kragujevac; later Belgrade</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Autonomous and then independent Serbian principality of the 19th century, built by the rival Obrenović and Karađorđević dynasties on the way to a modern kingdom.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the collapse of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/revolutionary-serbia">Revolutionary Serbia</a> in 1813, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milos-obrenovic">Miloš Obrenović</a> launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-serbian-uprising">Second Serbian Uprising</a> at Takovo in 1815. Combining fighting with negotiation, he won recognition as supreme prince in 1817, and an Ottoman decree of 1830 made <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a> an autonomous hereditary principality paying tribute, with Ottoman garrisons only in a few fortresses.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Miloš ruled autocratically, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sretenje-constitution">Sretenje Constitution</a> of 1835 and the Ottoman-imposed constitution of 1838 set up a council that curbed him. Power swung between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obrenovic-dynasty">Obrenović</a> princes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-karadordevic">Alexander Karađorđević</a>, who reigned from 1842 to 1858. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-obrenovic">Michael Obrenović</a> modernised the army and secured the departure of the last Ottoman garrisons in 1867. Peasant smallholders raised pigs for export to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/serbia">Serbia</a> fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1876 to 1878, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> in 1878 recognised its independence and added Niš and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pirot">Pirot</a>. In 1882 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan-i-of-serbia">Prince Milan Obrenović</a> proclaimed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-serbia.jpg" alt="Principality of Serbia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: This vector image includes elements that have been taken or adapted from this file · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Serbia_(1835%E2%80%931882).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1815 CE – 1882 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kragujevac; later Belgrade</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Miloš Obrenović, Michael Obrenović, Milan Obrenović</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Serbian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Serbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Serbia (1815-1882), Kneževina Srbija</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1848&amp;focus=principality-of-serbia" aria-label="Principality of Serbia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5181 619 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Serbia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5501 875L5488 850M5501 875L5709 970M5501 875L5488 850"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5488 850h0M5709 970h0M5488 850h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5501 875h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5501 875h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kragujevac. <a href="/atlas?year=1848&amp;focus=principality-of-serbia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
